<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kuruc</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1671">1671</a>–<a href="/years/1711">1711</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Kurucs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kuruc was the name given to the armed anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> rebels of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a> between 1671 and 1711. Drawn from Protestant nobles, lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>, haiduks and peasants, they fought against Habsburg absolutism and religious persecution, first under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-thokoly">Imre Thököly</a> and later in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii-rakoczi">Francis II Rákóczi</a>'s War of Independence, which ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-szatmar">Peace of Szatmár</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kuruc.jpg" alt="Kuruc" width="320" height="272" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Viktor Madarász · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madar%C3%A1sz_Kuruc_%C3%A9s_labanc_1855.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word kuruc was first used in 1514 for the armed peasants led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyorgy-dozsa">György Dózsa</a>. Today's etymologists derive it from the Turkish kurudsch, 'rebel' or 'insurgent'. In 1671 Meni, the beglerbeg pasha of Eger, applied it to the mostly noble refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a>, and from 1671 to 1711 it was used in Hungarian, Slovak and Turkish texts for the rebels against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>. The opposite term, labanc, widespread after 1678, meant the Austrians and their loyalist supporters; it comes from a Hungarian word for 'long hair', referring to the wigs of Austrian soldiers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> conspiracy and the rebellion of Francis I Rákóczi, Emperor Leopold I introduced absolutist government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-hungary">Royal Hungary</a>. The court tried again to suppress Protestants, and in 1673 it set up a governing council under Johann Caspar von Ampringen. Soldiers dismissed from frontier castles, nobles who lost their estates and Protestants fled east and gathered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>. In 1672 these 'fugitives' (bújdosók) invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a> and took the castles of Diósgyőr, Ónod, Szendrő and Tokaj, but on 26 October 1672 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> army defeated them at Gyurke. In 1674 a trial of Protestant pastors ended in sentences of death, and the pastors were sold as galley slaves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, which caused public outcry across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p>Between 1674 and 1678 the fugitives were led by Count Paul Wesselényi, and in May 1677 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> and the fugitives signed a treaty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a>. In 1678 Mihály Teleki took the lead, and the same year Emeric Thököly declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and his army occupied almost all of Lower and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-hungary">Upper Hungary</a>. The fugitives elected him their leader at Szoboszló in January 1680. In 1681 Leopold I restored the constitution and the Palatine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>From then on the history of the kurucs is that of the Thököly Uprising of 1680–85 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rakoczis-war-of-independence">Rákóczi's War of Independence</a> of 1703–1711, which had a wider social base and more complex aims than the original movements. Rákóczi himself did not use the word, preferring the French term insurgents or malcontents. After 1711 many former kuruc soldiers joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> army, and in the first half of the 18th century 'kuruc' came to mean Hungarian hussars in that service. By the end of the century it was only a historical term. Today it can denote Hungarians who stand for strict national independence, in a positive sense of patriotic or a negative sense of chauvinistic. The South German oath Kruzitürken joins Kuruzen and Türken.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kuruc” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Forces of the Habsburg Empire, Denmark-Norway and Vojvodian Serbs defeated the <b>Kuruc</b> army of Francis II Rákóczi at Zsibó in Transylvania.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-15">15 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Imre Thököly, a Lutheran noble, led the <b>Kuruc</b> rebels of northern Hungary against Habsburg persecution of Protestants from 1678.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-upper-hungary">Principality of Upper Hungary</a></li></ul></section>
